{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '1,220p' /home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/seed402918419/9/prompt.md\"","aggregated_output":"你是一个 ARC-AGI 谜题设计专家。下面的 20 道参考题是难度和机制质量的标杆。仔细阅读每道题的**解题逻辑**（解题者从样例归纳规则的认知过程），而非表面场景。你的新题应在机制深度和隐蔽性上与参考题持平或者实现超越。\n\n每个参考题描述包含：\n- `description`：一句话概述题目的变换目标。\n- `Input structure`：输入网格中有哪些对象、背景、标记、边界或组件。\n- `Rule`：从输入到输出的确定性变换规则。\n- `Key insight`：解题所需的核心洞见。\n\n参考题目如下：\n<reference_puzzle index=\"1\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"ac6f9922\" lines=\"18-40\">\n<description>\nSummarize the enclosed table slots by the single object color inside each slot, using the slot background color for empty slots.\n\nInput structure\n- The most common color forms the outer background and all enclosed slot\n  backgrounds.\n- The enclosed background components form a complete rectangular array of\n  slots, ordered by their positions.\n- Each slot contains either no other color or exactly one object color.\n\nRule\n- Find all 4-connected components of the most common color that do not touch\n  the input edge.\n- Sort their bounding boxes into row and column order to define the output\n  table.\n- For each slot, output its unique non-background color if present; otherwise\n  output the background color.\n\nKey insight\n- The large grid is an expanded table: the enclosed background components are\n  the cells, and any colored object drawn inside a cell is that cell's summary\n  value.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"2\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"8886d717\" lines=\"43-63\">\n<description>\nRepair markers embedded in the main object and copy the remaining markers one pixel toward the solid edge.\n\nInput structure:\n- A mostly background grid contains one solid non-background edge line.\n- Apart from that edge and the background, there is one large main object\n  color and a smaller marker color.\n- Each marker is either embedded in the main object, touching it on at\n  least three 4-neighbor sides, or isolated on the background.\n\nRule:\n- Embedded markers are recolored to the main object color.\n- Isolated background markers remain in place and also paint the adjacent\n  background pixel in the direction of the solid edge line.\n- All other pixels are unchanged.\n\nKey insight:\nThe full edge line is a direction cue: it tells which way the background\nmarkers extend, while markers surrounded by the main object are simply\ndefects to be filled.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"3\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"ba1aa698\" lines=\"41-55\">\n<description>\nExtrapolate the moving sprite from a horizontal sequence of shared-border frames into the next standalone frame.\n\nInput structure\n- The grid is a row of equal-width framed panels sharing their vertical border columns.\n- Each panel has the same fill color and one identical interior sprite at the same local columns.\n- The sprite's top row changes by a constant amount from panel to panel.\n\nRule\n- Build one output panel with the same height, frame color, fill color, and sprite.\n- Continue the sprite's diagonal motion one more panel to the right.\n- Because the output is a standalone panel, advance by the full panel width, not the shared-border spacing.\n\nKey insight\n- The panels are consecutive frames of motion; the output is the next frame after the sequence.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"4\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"b9e38dc0\" lines=\"7-18\">\n<description>\nFill the region enclosed by a wall sprite and radiate color outward through its opening.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = most common color. Wall = most frequent non-bg color, forming a partial enclosure (e.g. U-shape).\n- A fill-color pixel sits inside the enclosed area. Other non-bg pixels may act as obstacles.\nRule:\n- Flood-fill the enclosed area with the fill color, radiating outward through the wall's open end.\n- Obstacles cast \"shadows\" along the radiate direction, blocking fill behind them.\n\nKey insight: The wall endpoints define the opening direction; fill radiates outward through it, and obstacles block propagation along that same axis.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"5\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"8a6d367c\" lines=\"52-67\">\n<description>\nComplete the framed area by expanding the unique upper template that contains the existing seed pixels.\n\nInput structure:\n- A teal background contains small colored template masks above a larger rectangular frame.\n- The frame encloses one fill color arranged as a partial block-scaled copy of one template.\n- The frame interior dimensions are an integer square scale of the matching template bbox.\n\nRule:\n- Crop to the rectangular frame.\n- Identify the unique upper template whose block-scaled mask covers every existing fill pixel inside the frame.\n- Reset the frame interior to background and paint the whole scaled mask with the fill color.\n\nKey insight:\nThe lower colored pixels are seed cells that reveal which upper template should be completed inside the frame.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"6\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"278e5215\" lines=\"51-66\">\n<description>\nRecolor the large mask crop column-by-column using the separate three-row palette strip.\n\nInput structure:\n- The background is the most common color.\n- One large monochrome foreground object is the mask; its bbox is the output region and may contain background holes.\n- The remaining foreground is a 3-row palette rectangle with the same width as the mask bbox: two identical color rows and one uniform row.\n\nRule:\n- Crop to the mask object's bbox.\n- For each output column, color mask pixels with the corresponding color from the repeated palette rows.\n- Replace background holes inside the crop with the palette's uniform third-row color.\n\nKey insight:\nThe grey object is only a stencil; its columns select colors from the legend, while its holes expose the legend's fill color.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"7\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"f28a3cbb\" lines=\"42-50\">\n<description>\nCompress each colored ray pixel inward until it touches that color's 3x3 anchor block.\nInput structure\n- A uniform background grid with non-background colors.\n- Each foreground color contains one solid 3x3 anchor block; all other pixels of that color lie on straight 8-neighbor rays aimed at that block.\nRule\n- Keep anchor blocks fixed, erase loose foreground pixels, and for each ray pack its pixels into consecutive cells adjacent to the anchor along the same ray.\nKey insight\n- The scattered pixels are displaced pixels on visible row, column, or diagonal sightlines back to their same-colored 3x3 block.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"8\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"80a900e0\" lines=\"69-82\">\n<description>\nExtend diagonal rays outward from outer-border segments adjacent to diamond shapes on a checkerboard.\n\nInput structure:\n- Checkerboard bg (two most common colors).\n- Hollow diamond outlines (8-pixel) in a non-bg color (inner border).\n- Short diagonal line segments in another non-bg color adjacent to each diamond edge (outer border).\n\nRule:\n- For each outer-border segment, determine the outward direction (perpendicular to the segment, away from the diamond).\n- From each endpoint of the segment, cast a diagonal ray outward, painting over bg pixels with the segment's color until hitting a non-bg pixel or the grid edge.\n\nKey insight: Rays emit only from the two endpoints of each outer-border segment, not every pixel, and travel diagonally away from the diamond.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"9\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"d968ffd4\" lines=\"15-28\">\n<description>\nExtend each of two aligned rectangular marker objects toward the other, filling half of the gap across the full perpendicular span.\n\nInput structure\n- A dominant background contains exactly two solid, same-sized monochromatic rectangles.\n- The rectangles share either the same row span or the same column span, with a background gap between their facing edges.\n\nRule\n- If the rectangles are side-by-side, color the left half of the inter-object gap across every row with the left object's color, and the right half with the right object's color.\n- If one rectangle is above the other, color the upper half of the gap across every column with the upper object's color, and the lower half with the lower object's color.\n- When the gap length is odd, leave the center row/column as background.\n\nKey insight\n- The marker objects are endpoints of an axial corridor; their own size identifies the facing edge, while the filled corridor spans the whole grid perpendicular to that axis.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"10\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"e87109e9\" lines=\"68-83\">\n<description>\nEmit four teal beams from a square, turning at colored obstacles per a legend.\n\nInput structure:\n- Top band: grey (5) legend with colored vertical bars in cells; bar position (left or right) encodes turn direction.\n- Below: main grid with bg, a solid teal (8) square, and colored obstacle blocks.\n\nRule:\n- Remove the legend from the output.\n- From the teal square, shoot a beam in each cardinal direction (as a sliding copy of the square).\n- Each beam paints teal over bg cells as it moves.\n- On hitting an obstacle of color C, consult the legend: bar on left → turn counterclockwise, bar on right → turn clockwise. Continue in the new direction.\n- Beams stop at grid edges.\n\nKey insight: The legend's bar position (left vs right within its cell) is the encoding for turn direction at each obstacle color.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"11\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"5751f35e\" lines=\"29-43\">\n<description>\nComplete sparse nested rectangular color layers into solid rectangles.\n\nInput structure\nThe background is the dominant color, and every foreground color marks one\naxis-aligned rectangular layer; the layer bboxes have unique areas and are\nnested from outermost to innermost.\nRule\nTake each color's bbox as its completed rectangle, except that a child bbox\nwhich already matches at least three sides of its parent's one-pixel inset\nexpands to that full inset; repaint the rectangles largest to smallest.\nKey insight\nThe scattered pixels are samples of nested rectangular regions, so the\noutput is a clean redraw of those regions with inner colors overwriting\nouter colors.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"12\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"b71a7747\" lines=\"10-23\">\n<description>\nExtract the hidden two-color image by compacting the rows and columns of its complete mask.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
